Hi!

Most of these warnings are common and addressed here:

BioGeoBEARS Warnings to Ignore Mostly
http://phylo.wikidot.com/biogeobears-warnings-to-ignore-mostly#unused_control_arguments

The one about "NaNs produced in likelihood calculation" means that some sampled parameter values are producing NaNs.  Probably this is something like a short branch + a low rate parameter causing tiny probability numbers that cause underflow errors in the computer (which could cause 0 or negative probabilities).

As the warning message says, "you specified that BioGeoBEARS_run_object$on_NaN_error=-1e+50", so this means that a log-likelihood (lnL) of "NaN" is being changed into a lnL of -1e+50, so that the optimizer doesn't crash.  Common lnLs are -50, -100, etc., so an lnL of -1e+50 just tells the optimizer "these parameter values cause super-low likelihood, avoid this region of parameter space".

In particular, I have noticed that GenSA hits this warning sometimes, probably because it is doing a more thorough search, sometimes hits these parameter values, probably because it is more carefully searching at the edges of the user-specified parameter limits.

Short version: Basically you can ignore that warning if the optimization seems to be working otherwise.

But, as always, it is the researcher's responsibility to THINK about their optimization results and BE ALERT to potential signs of problems:

http://phylo.wikidot.com/biogeobears-warnings-to-ignore-mostly#different_scalings

Advice On Statistical Model Comparison In BioGeoBEARS: ML optimization routines and their pitfalls
http://phylo.wikidot.com/advice-on-statistical-model-comparison-in-biogeobears#ML_optimx

BioGeoBEARS Mistakes To Avoid: Issues with Maximum Likelihood (ML) optimization
http://phylo.wikidot.com/biogeobears-mistakes-to-avoid#optim

Cheers,
Nick

